//? Title: CRC-32: Actual Byte Folds, Error Syndromes and a Collision
//? Subtitle: Reflected IEEE parameters, the original 152-bit experiment, and a fifteen-bit counterexample to an overbroad guarantee
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A CRC is a polynomial error detector whose guarantees depend on its exact
generator, conditioning and bit order. We retain the standard 123456789 check
and the original nineteen-byte message, but replace broad claims about odd-bit
errors and avalanche with inspectable native evidence. The same production
kernel exposes actual table addresses and raw registers; a bounded census
checks every single-bit corruption and its output difference. Polynomial
linearity explains why those differences ignore the message payload at fixed
length. It also supplies a concrete collision: a 33-bit burst containing
fifteen flips leaves this CRC unchanged. The distinction between guaranteed
short-burst detection and general collision resistance is visible in the
numbers rather than hidden behind an average changed-bit count.
)

\toc

> Specify the CRC before interpreting a number

This report uses the reflected IEEE CRC-32 convention, also called
CRC-32/ISO-HDLC: width 32, normal polynomial 0x04C11DB7 with its leading
x³² term implicit, reflected polynomial 0xEDB88320, initial raw register
0xFFFFFFFF and final XOR 0xFFFFFFFF. Each byte is consumed least-significant
bit first. Empty input has finalized CRC zero. Embedded zero bytes are data,
not string terminators. This identifies the calculation; it is not a claim
that every format uses the same byte serialization or covered fields.

The generator is

\[
G(x)=x^{32}+x^{26}+x^{23}+x^{22}+x^{16}+x^{12}+x^{11}+x^{10}
+x^8+x^7+x^5+x^4+x^2+x+1.
\]
\alttext(The degree-thirty-two generator has fifteen nonzero coefficients, including its leading term and constant one.)

The raw register is an internal uncomplemented state. The public CRC is the
raw register XOR 0xFFFFFFFF. Confusing the two changes every displayed value.
The usual polynomial-division picture describes the linear remainder core;
this public convention also includes initial and final conditioning.

One byte fold uses j=(raw XOR byte) AND 255, followed by
raw_next=(raw>>8) XOR table[j]. The immutable table encodes eight reflected
shift/conditional-XOR reductions. The following program prints all nine real
byte updates and diagrams the first one, instead of reconstructing a second
algorithm in the report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crc32_byte_fold.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::crc32_trace("123456789",9);
  if(t.checksum!=0xCBF43926u || t.raw!=0x340BC6D9u || t.steps.size()!=9) return 1;
  std::print("| byte index | unsigned byte | raw before | table address | actual table entry | raw after |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) std::print("| {} | {:02X} | {:08X} | {:02X} | {:08X} | {:08X} |\n",s.index,s.byte,s.raw_before,s.lookup_index,s.table_value,s.raw_after);
  const auto& first=t.steps.front();
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.8;box.height=1.4;box.font_size=17;
  box.fill={219,234,254};
  d.node("raw",{0,0},std::format("raw before\n{:08X}",first.raw_before),box);
  d.node("byte",{0,-2.2},std::format("input byte\n{:02X}",first.byte),box);
  box.fill={254,243,199};
  d.node("address",{4,-2.2},std::format("low-byte address\n{:02X}",first.lookup_index),box);
  d.node("shift",{4,0},std::format("raw shifted by 8\n{:08X}",first.raw_before>>8),box);
  box.fill={220,252,231};
  d.node("table",{8,-2.2},std::format("table entry\n{:08X}",first.table_value),box);
  d.node("after",{12,-1.1},std::format("XOR gives raw after\n{:08X}",first.raw_after),box);
  d.edge("raw","shift").edge("raw","address").edge("byte","address")
   .edge("address","table").edge("shift","after").edge("table","after")
   .text({6,2},"First actual byte fold of the nine-byte standard check",23)
   .text({6,-4.1},std::format("After all nine bytes: raw {:08X}; finalized CRC {:08X}",t.raw,t.checksum),20)
   .save_svg("build/crc32_byte_fold.svg");
  std::print("\nStandard check CBF43926; actual final raw register 340BC6D9.\n");
}
```
