Standard check CBF43926; actual final raw register 340BC6D9.
\image(figures/crc32_byte_fold.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first actual lookup uses byte 0x31, address 0xCE and table value 0x7CDCEFB7. XOR with shifted raw 0x00FFFFFF gives raw 0x7C231048; the full nine-byte result is finalized separately.)
\alttext(A native dependency diagram splits the raw register into a right-shift branch and a low-byte lookup branch, incorporates input byte thirty-one hexadecimal, and combines the two branches by XOR.)

The table is generated at compile time. crc32() uses one table lookup per byte,
O(n) time and O(1) state beyond that fixed table. crc32_trace() shares this
executing fold and owns n event records, so it uses O(n) storage. It is the
same production byte algorithm, not a separate illustrative bit loop.

> Error syndromes are linear after equal-length conditioning cancels

For messages of a fixed length b bits, write the public CRC as C(M)=L_b(M) XOR c_b,
where L_b is the linear polynomial remainder mapping and c_b is the conditioning
constant. If error pattern E toggles bits, then

\[
C(M\mathbin{\oplus}E)\mathbin{\oplus}C(M)=L_b(E).
\]
\alttext(The XOR difference of the corrupted and original CRC equals the linear error syndrome; the fixed-length conditioning constants cancel.)

Thus the syndrome of one bit position depends on its position and total message
length, not on which letters happen to be in the message. For two errors, their
syndromes XOR. This is useful structure for error detection, and also means an
average output-bit count cannot establish a cryptographic avalanche property.

Retain the original message The quick brown fox: nineteen bytes, or 152 bits.
Bit b means byte b/8, mask 1<<(b%8). Every candidate corruption starts from the
same original bytes; mutations do not accumulate across the census.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crc32_avalanche.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <set>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string message="The quick brown fox";
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::crc32_flip_scan(message,2907);
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::crc32_flip_scan(std::string(message.size(),'\0'),2907);
  if(a.checksum!=0xB74574DEu || a.detected!=152 || a.flips.size()!=152) return 1;
  std::vector<double> positions,changed;
  std::set<std::uint32_t> syndromes;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<a.flips.size();++i) {
    if(a.flips[i].syndrome!=zero.flips[i].syndrome) return 1;
    positions.push_back(a.flips[i].bit);changed.push_back(a.flips[i].changed_bits);
    syndromes.insert(a.flips[i].syndrome);
  }
  if(syndromes.size()!=152) return 1;
  std::print("| original CRC | one-bit errors detected | mean output bits changed | minimum | maximum | unique one-bit syndromes |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| {:08X} | {} / {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a.checksum,a.detected,a.flips.size(),a.mean_changed_bits,a.minimum_changed_bits,a.maximum_changed_bits,syndromes.size());
  std::print("\nAll 152 syndromes equal those for nineteen zero bytes. Distinct one-bit syndromes imply all 11476 two-bit errors in this fixture are detected.\n");
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(positions,changed,"changed output bits",{37,99,235},3)
      .abline_h(16,{220,38,38},1.5)
      .legend({"syndrome popcount","16: half the width"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Original message: 152 independent one-bit CRC differences")
      .x_label("input bit, byte-major and LSB-first").y_label("set bits in the 32-bit XOR syndrome")
      .x_limits(-2,154).y_limits(0,32).size(900,560)
      .save_svg("build/crc32_avalanche.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| original CRC | one-bit errors detected | mean output bits changed | minimum | maximum | unique one-bit syndromes |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| B74574DE | 152 / 152 | 15.460526 | 10 | 23 | 152 |

All 152 syndromes equal those for nineteen zero bytes. Distinct one-bit syndromes imply all 11476 two-bit errors in this fixture are detected.
\image(figures/crc32_avalanche.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original changed-bit scatter is retained, with a reference line at sixteen rather than an asserted ideal avalanche law. These are position-dependent deterministic syndromes.)
\alttext(A scatterplot shows the number of changed CRC bits for each of the 152 independently flipped input positions. A horizontal line marks half the output width.)

Every single-bit error is detected here. The mean is 15.460526 of 32 bits,
matching the old experiment, but that average is not an accidental-collision
probability. Uniqueness of the 152 single-bit syndromes establishes detection
of all 152×151/2=11,476 two-bit errors for this particular length and polynomial.
A blanket all-length double-error guarantee needs a separation/order argument:
a two-bit error is undetected exactly when G divides x^d+1, where d is the
separation. This finite census does not prove an unbounded statement.

> The short-burst guarantee, and its exact boundary

A burst's length is the span from its first changed bit through its last,
including both endpoints; bits inside that span may be zero. A nonzero burst
of length B is x^j Q(x), with degree(Q)=B−1 and nonzero endpoints.
Because G has constant term one, it is coprime to x. For B≤32, Q has degree
at most 31, so degree-32 G cannot divide it. Therefore such a burst has a
nonzero syndrome. This is an algebraic guarantee for the specified CRC error
model, independent of the original payload.

At length 33, Q can be G itself, whose remainder is zero. G has fifteen
nonzero coefficients, so this undetected error has odd weight. Equivalently,
G(1)=1 in GF(2), so G lacks the factor x+1 that would guarantee detection of
all odd-weight errors. The former report's all-odd-error claim is false for
this polynomial.

To map the normal polynomial into the reflected byte-major bit stream, use
positions 32−e for each generator exponent e. The resulting 33-bit span has
changed positions [0,6,9,10,16,20,21,22,24,25,27,28,30,31,32]. On five zero
bytes it produces the binary byte pattern 41 06 71 DB 01 in hexadecimal.
The next program applies exactly those native bit toggles to the original
message, then checks the collision and diagrams the actual changed bits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crc32_generator_collision.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string original="The quick brown fox";
  const std::vector<std::size_t> bits{0,6,9,10,16,20,21,22,24,25,27,28,30,31,32};
  const auto changed=dm::algorithms::crc32_flip_bits(original,bits);
  const auto before=dm::algorithms::crc32(original),after=dm::algorithms::crc32(changed);
  if(original==changed || before!=after || bits.size()!=15) return 1;
  std::print("| changed bits | burst span | original CRC | changed CRC | XOR syndrome |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  std::print("| {} | 33 | {:08X} | {:08X} | {:08X} |\n",bits.size(),before,after,before^after);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.05;box.height=1.05;box.font_size=16;
  for(std::size_t bit=0;bit<33;++bit) {
    const auto difference=static_cast<unsigned char>(original[bit/8])^static_cast<unsigned char>(changed[bit/8]);
    const bool toggle=(difference&(1u<<(bit%8)))!=0;
    box.fill=toggle?dm::graphics::RGB{254,215,170}:dm::graphics::RGB{243,244,246};
    d.node(std::format("bit{}",bit),{1.3*(bit%11),-1.5*(bit/11)},std::format("bit {}\n{}",bit,toggle?1:0),box);
  }
  d.text({6.5,1.8},"A 33-bit burst with fifteen flips and zero syndrome",23)
   .text({6.5,-4.8},std::format("Original CRC {:08X} = changed CRC {:08X}",before,after),21)
   .save_svg("build/crc32_generator_collision.svg");
  bool trace_cap=false,work_cap=false,invalid=false;
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::crc32_trace("abc",2); }
  catch(const std::length_error&) { trace_cap=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::crc32_flip_scan(original,2906); }
  catch(const std::length_error&) { work_cap=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::crc32_flip_bits("A",{0,8}); }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { invalid=true; }
  if(!trace_cap || !work_cap || !invalid) return 1;
  std::print("\nComplete trace cap, exact scan-work cap and invalid-bit validation: pass.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| changed bits | burst span | original CRC | changed CRC | XOR syndrome |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 15 | 33 | B74574DE | B74574DE | 00000000 |

Complete trace cap, exact scan-work cap and invalid-bit validation: pass.
\image(figures/crc32_generator_collision.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Fifteen orange cells mark actual toggles in a 33-bit span. The two different messages have identical CRC B74574DE, so neither odd weight nor a changed-bit average prevents this structured collision.)
\alttext(Thirty-three indexed cells in three rows show the generator error pattern, with fifteen orange one cells and grey zeros. The displayed original and changed CRC values are identical.)

CRC therefore supports accidental-error detection with specific algebraic
guarantees, rather than authentication of an adversarial message. It is not
a substitute for a message authentication code. The collision above is a
direct consequence of its public linear structure, not a random search.

> Costs, ownership and reproducible controls

The actual byte trace defaults to a complete cap of 1,000,000 bytes/events.
The corruption census recomputes n bytes for one baseline and each of 8n
independent flips, so it performs exactly n(8n+1) byte folds: 2,907 for this
nineteen-byte message. Its default budget is 100,000,000 folds, excluding
scratch copies, bit toggles, population counts and output allocation. Runtime
is O(n+8n²), with O(n+8n) owned storage. One scratch message is toggled and
restored between trials; no report-side CRC or popcount implementation remains.

Complete trace/work and size-arithmetic checks occur before allocation or CRC
evaluation, and results never silently truncate. A zero optional cap disables
only that cap. Requested bit addresses validate in full before the input is
copied; duplicate addresses cancel in pairs. All returned traces, changed bytes
and census records own their storage. Empty input has checksum zero, no flips,
false has_flips and zero summary placeholders. Allocation may still fail below
a cap. The original crc32() signature and checksums.hpp include path remain
available; independent calls share only immutable table constants.

No external dataset is useful here: the standard check, exact binary controls
and original fixed message make the convention and failure modes reproducible.
Native tests compare every one- and two-byte message with an independent
bit-at-a-time reference, plus 1,000 random binary messages and the existing
streaming codec CRC. They verify actual byte events, all original one-/two-bit
errors, payload-independent syndrome composition, every burst through width
twelve on an eight-byte frame and sampled bursts through width thirty-two.
The fifteen-bit generator collision holds at every fitting offset of the
original frame. Empty/binary data, complete budgets, invalid/duplicate bit
addresses and owned storage are covered, including address/undefined sanitizers.
These controls supplement the short-burst proof; they do not convert a finite
sample into a universal low-weight-distance claim.

> Primary reference

The [W3C PNG specification, Section 5.5 and Appendix D](https://www.w3.org/TR/png/)
specifies the polynomial, reflected bit order, initial/final conditioning and
sample table update. The report's burst proof and explicit generator collision
explain the consequences of those parameters. PNG additionally specifies its
covered chunk bytes and CRC serialization; this generic checksum API returns
the integer and leaves format framing to its caller.
